title: Trash on-disk layout
tags: Trash
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: Blobs and a small LMDB index under the repository's internal/trash/, and what an entry holds.

```
.metafolder/internal/trash/<id>    the trashed file, symlink or directory (the blob)
.metafolder/internal/trash/index/  the entry index (LMDB)
```

`<id>` is a fresh 32-character lowercase hex uuid; the blob has no extension (the
original name is in the index). One flat directory. `internal/` is excluded from
tracking, like the repository's store. The clients find it through the
`internal_dir` of `GET /repos/:repo`.

The index is a small dedicated key-value store — not the daemon's store, which
the daemon holds under an exclusive lock: the trash is client state, opened
directly by `mf` and the GUI. LMDB gives locking between the two, atomic writes
and corruption resistance; a process opens it once and shares it between its
commands. Two tables:

|!Table |!Key |!Value |
|`entries` |`<id>` |the entry, as JSON |
|`by_time` |`trashed_at` (big-endian) then `<id>` |nothing: the order of `list`, and the walk of `prune` |

An entry holds:

|!Field |!Meaning |
|`id` |the blob's name |
|`original_path` |the absolute path it was trashed from, as its exact bytes: the restore target |
|`original_name` |its basename as shown — an undecodable byte as `%XX` —, for display only |
|`trashed_at` |unix milliseconds |
|`size` |bytes; for a directory, the recursive total of its regular files |
|`is_dir` |the blob is a whole subtree |
|`reason` |`manual`, `revert` (a file a move would have overwritten — `rollback` before October 2026, read as `revert`) or `sync` |
|`revision` |the operation that trashed it, when there is one |
|`metarecord` |the metarecord it belonged to — how an undo finds the entry |
|`version` |that metarecord's version when trashed |
|`subtree` |the captured metarecords: `uuid`, `parent`, `name` (its `mfr_path` name, exact), `fields` (as the daemon renders them), `trashed` (deleted with the entry, or an ancestor recorded for context) |

A path or a name takes the wire form of [[Tree names]]: a string while it is text,
`{"text", "bytes"}` otherwise. A path that is not text is restored at its bytes, and a
name that is not text is captured like any other (it used to be dropped, leaving its
metarecord behind). An entry written before November 2026 holds strings, which read as
the same text: nothing to convert — but a name that was not text was recorded lossily
then, and its entry restores to that lossy name.

The trashing finds what to capture by the target's ''uuid'' and its ''exact'' path
(`tree/resolve-path` with `form: "verbatim"`), never by a query on the text of its
path, which a sibling really named `caf%E9` would also match.

The fields are kept as JSON, unlike field rows and log snapshots, which the
daemon splits into typed columns because it queries them. Nothing queries these:
they leave the daemon as JSON and go back to it as JSON.

The blob is moved before the entry is written, so an entry always implies its
blob; a failure in between leaks an unindexed blob, which `mf trash prune --all`
sweeps.

`reason`, `revision` and `version` are provenance. `metarecord` is what a
revert looks an entry up by; `version` also matches an entry written
before the metarecords were captured, whose deletion the log recorded as the
watcher's `file_deleted`.

The shared code is `metafolder_core::trash` (`TrashDir`, the capture, the
restore); each client adds its own glue.
